.TH RDMAPIPE 1 "August 2026" "rdmapipe 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rdmapipe \- connect Unix byte streams across an RDMA fabric
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rdmapipe
[OPTIONS] \fB--send\fR
.br
.B rdmapipe
[OPTIONS] \fB--recv\fR
.br
.B rdmapipe
[OPTIONS] HOST \fB--\fR COMMAND [ARG ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rdmapipe
transports local standard input to a remote standard output or command
standard input over one or two libibverbs reliable-connected channels.
SSH authenticates and launches the remote process; RDMA carries the
unencrypted, unchecksummed bulk byte stream.
.PP
The raw form is:
.PP
.nf
producer | rdmapipe --send |
  ssh host 'rdmapipe --recv | consumer'
.fi
.PP
The sender writes one newline-terminated JSON rendezvous descriptor to
standard output and keeps that descriptor pipe open while transferring.  The
receiver reads exactly one line and connects immediately.  Bulk bytes never
traverse the descriptor pipe.
.PP
The shorthand form is:
.PP
.nf
producer | rdmapipe host -- consumer args
.fi
.PP
The literal
.B --
is required, all rdmapipe options precede HOST, and everything following it is
the remote argv.
.PP
It supervises the equivalent sender and SSH receiver.  Command arguments are
transmitted as a base64-encoded, NUL-separated argv vector and passed to
.BR execvp (3),
not concatenated into shell text.  Invoke
.B sh -c
explicitly when shell syntax is wanted.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B --send
Run the descriptor-producing, standard-input-consuming sender.
.TP
.B --recv
Read one descriptor and write the received stream.  This mode also supports
the internal safe-argv execution used by shorthand.
.SH TRANSPORT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --channels= auto|1|2
Select the RC channel count.  Auto is the default and requires only one usable
path.  Two is selected when both peers expose useful paths or one 400-Gb/s
device faces two 200-Gb/s devices.
.TP
.BR --rails= auto|1|2
Alias for
.BR --channels .
.TP
.BI --chunk-size= SIZE
Set registered payload bytes per slot.  The default is 2M.  Values from 4K to
8M, aligned to 64 bytes, are accepted; K, M, and G are binary suffixes.
.TP
.BI --queue-depth= N
Set slots per channel.  The default is 4 and the accepted range is 2 through
4096.  Default payload memory is approximately 8 MiB per channel.
.TP
.BI --device= LIST
Restrict local discovery to comma-separated verbs device names, network device
names, or RoCE IPv4 addresses.
.TP
.BI --remote-device= LIST
Apply a receiver discovery filter in shorthand mode.
.TP
.BI --port= PORT
Set the sender's first TCP bootstrap port.  Zero, the default, requests
ephemeral ports.  Fixed ports advance for additional advertised endpoints.
.TP
.BI --timeout= SECONDS
Set the overall descriptor/setup and no-progress timeout.  The default is 300;
the accepted range is 1 through 86400.
.TP
.B --discard
Receive and validate the complete framed stream without writing its payload.
This is a receiver benchmark mode.
.TP
.B --remote-discard
Enable receiver discard mode from shorthand.
.SH SSH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --remote-path= PROGRAM
Set the remote rdmapipe executable.  The default is
.BR rdmapipe .
For a per-user install, quote the local argument as
.BR "--remote-path='~/.local/bin/rdmapipe'"
so the remote login shell expands the tilde.
.TP
.BI --ssh= PROGRAM
Set the SSH executable.  The default is
.BR ssh .
.TP
.BI --ssh-option= ARG
Append one local SSH argument before HOST.  Repeat as necessary.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.BR -q , " --quiet"
Suppress topology and final statistics.  Errors are not suppressed.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Add endpoint and lifecycle detail on standard error.  Repeat for more detail.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Print concise command help.
.TP
.B --version
Print program/protocol versions and compiled defaults.
.SH STREAM SEMANTICS
The sender reads directly into fixed registered slots.  The receiver writes
directly from completed registered slots and reposts a slot only after its
payload is consumed.  This provides bounded backpressure.
.PP
On Linux, the sender best-effort grows an input pipe, temporarily enables
nonblocking reads, drains immediately available input before polling again,
and restores the original file status flags after the transfer.
.PP
EOF is an explicit FIN frame.  Disconnect before FIN, invalid ordering, write
failure, early consumer exit, timeout, authentication failure, and QP failure
are errors.  Version 1 is unidirectional; remote stdout and stderr return over
SSH in shorthand mode.
.PP
Shorthand consumers run in a separate process group.  Transfer failure or
cancellation signals the complete command tree; a command that ignores
SIGTERM is killed after a two-second cleanup grace period.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stream and remote command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line or descriptor syntax error.
.TP
.B 69
No usable RDMA endpoint or connection is available.
.TP
.B 70
Protocol, authentication, ordering, or explicit-FIN error.
.TP
.B 74
Standard I/O, verbs, or control-channel error.
.TP
.B 75
Setup or progress timeout.
.TP
.B 126, 127
Remote command could not be executed or found.
.TP
.B 128+N
Terminated by signal N.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RDMAPIPE_SSH
Default shorthand SSH executable.
.TP
.B RDMAPIPE_REMOTE_PATH
Default remote rdmapipe executable.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
docker image save --platform linux/arm64 app:latest |
  rdmapipe ostrich -- sudo docker image load

tar -C /models -cf - glmrt |
  rdmapipe kiwi -- tar -xf - -C /srv/models

set -o pipefail
producer | rdmapipe --send |
  ssh dodo 'set -o pipefail; rdmapipe --recv | consumer'
.fi
.SH SECURITY
The descriptor contains a random one-shot token used to authenticate bootstrap
connections.  Neither RDMA payload nor TCP control is encrypted or
cryptographically checksummed.  Use rdmapipe only on an appropriately trusted
fabric or compose application security explicitly.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR pipe (7),
.BR ibv_post_send (3),
.BR ibv_post_recv (3)
